<header class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Security</h1>
        <p>How we keep each institution's data separate, protect accounts and exam records, and what we haven't done yet.</p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="wrap prose">
    <p class="updated">Last updated 21 November 2026.</p>

    <h2 id="isolation">Each institution's data stays its own</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Every institution is a separate organization. Every database query is filtered to the signed-in person's
            organization, and the database refuses writes outside it.</li>
        <li>Everything requires a sign-in unless it is deliberately public (these pages, sign-in and health checks), and every
            staff page checks the person's role.</li>
        <li>Instructors can be limited to the courses and exams they created.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="accounts">Accounts and sign-in</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Passwords are hashed with Argon2id, with lockout and back-off after repeated failed sign-ins.</li>
        <li>Staff must use two-step verification with an authenticator app.</li>
        <li>Removing a member or changing their role takes effect within about a minute, not at their next sign-in.</li>
        <li>New members join by email invitation, with single-use links that expire.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="records">Exam records you can trust</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>A student's original answer can't be rewritten. Corrections are stored beside it.</li>
        <li>The audit log is append-only: grade decisions, approvals, admin changes and denied access are recorded and can't be edited or deleted.</li>
        <li>AI grades are preliminary. A person grades, and an authorized person approves before a student sees anything.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="data">Protecting data</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Hosted on Microsoft Azure. Traffic is encrypted with HTTPS, and browsers are told to use it every time.</li>
        <li>Secrets and provider keys are kept in the hosting settings and Azure Key Vault, not in the code. The keys that protect sign-in cookies are stored encrypted by a Key Vault key.</li>
        <li>Video never leaves the student's device. Only short descriptions of camera events are sent.</li>
        <li>Logs carry record identifiers only, never answers, keys, tokens or passwords.</li>
        <li>Institutions set their own retention period, and can export or erase a student's records.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="integrations">Integrations</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>API keys are long random secrets; only a hash is stored, and they can be revoked at any time.</li>
        <li>Webhooks are signed, and deliveries only go to public HTTPS addresses, checked again at every delivery.</li>
        <li>The API, the exam connection and sign-in are rate limited.</li>
        <li>Uploads are limited by type, size and page count, and checked to be what their name says.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="ai">AI-specific risks</h2>
    <p>Students' answers are passed to the AI panel as data to be judged, with instructions to ignore anything in them
        that tries to give orders. That reduces the risk of a student talking the panel into a better grade; it does not
        remove it. The controls that hold regardless are the human grade, the approval step, and a flag that sends answers
        addressed to the grader for review.</p>

    <h2 id="review">How we check</h2>
    <p>The platform was reviewed against the OWASP Application Security Verification Standard (version 4.0, level 2) in
        November 2026. That review was internal, not an independent audit. Every high and medium issue it found has been fixed. We have no third-party certification yet. We will publish one here when we have it, and we're happy to
        answer an institution's security questionnaire.</p>

    <h2 id="report">Report a vulnerability</h2>
    <p>If you think you have found a security problem, tell us through the <a href="contact">contact form</a> (choose
        "Something else" and start your message with "Security"). Please give us a reasonable time to fix it before telling
        anyone else, and don't access other people's data or disrupt the service while testing. We won't take action
        against anyone who reports a problem in good faith this way.</p>
</div>
